  <adoption-note date="2026-08-28">
    ADOPTED INTO THIS PROJECT, NOT DEFERRED. Owner direction 2026-08-28: "if an issue is
    important, then it should be added to this project NOT deferred to others."

    The check lives in ci-tier2-advisory.yml, built by THIS PROJECT. A 40x disagreement between CI and local means a developer cannot reproduce it, therefore cannot fix it, therefore learns to ignore it. That is exactly the "constant reds" failure mode north star #3 forbids.

    This is a DEFECT REPORT AGAINST THIS PROJECT OWN DELIVERABLE, not new scope. The project
    cannot honestly close while something it shipped is known-broken. That is north star #1
    ("fix it right the first time") and #3 ("no constant reds").
  </adoption-note>

  <goal>A developer running the documented local command sees the same file count CI sees, so the check is actionable.</goal>

  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="1">Diagnose the 40x gap BEFORE changing anything. Candidates: glob expansion differing between bash and PowerShell; .prettierignore not applied on one path; line-ending normalization counting every file as changed; CI scanning build output that local excludes.</step>
    <step order="2">CRLF is the leading suspect on this repo -- .gitattributes and core.autocrlf interact with prettier endOfLine. Verify before assuming.</step>
    <step order="3">Fix the root cause, NOT the count. Do not narrow the CI glob to make the numbers match; that hides the disagreement rather than resolving it.</step>
    <step order="4">Print the exact local reproduction command in the job output so the next person does not re-derive it.</step>
  </steps>

  <notes date="2026-08-28">
    COMPLETE. Full write-up: notes/092-prettier-reproducibility.md.

    ROOT CAUSE: .prettierrc.json declared "endOfLine": "crlf", backed by NOTHING. .editorconfig
    scopes end_of_line=crlf to [*.{cs,csx,vb,vbx}] only; .gitattributes has text eol=crlf for those
    same C# types only (added 2026-08-19 to fix THIS EXACT BUG CLASS for dotnet format, with a
    comment describing the identical symptom). TypeScript had no declared line-ending policy at all.
    Windows dev (autocrlf=true) => CRLF tree => 0 of 1911 flagged. Linux CI => LF stored bytes =>
    1907 of 1911 flagged. CI was failing essentially every .ts/.tsx in src/client on line endings.

    PROVEN, NOT GUESSED: the first isolated two-line fixture appeared to REFUTE the line-ending
    hypothesis (both CRLF and LF passed) -- that test was too trivial to be valid. The decisive
    test inverted the setting against the real corpus: `--end-of-line lf` against the CRLF working
    tree flagged 1911, the exact mirror of CI's 1907. Sole discriminator confirmed.

    FIX: "endOfLine": "auto". Line endings are then governed by Git (core.autocrlf + .gitattributes)
    rather than asserted independently by the formatter for a type the repo never declared a policy
    for. Verified 0 differences on BOTH a CRLF working tree and a real subtree copy converted to LF.

    REJECTED the .cs precedent (adding `*.ts text eol=crlf`): that precedent exists to BACK an
    already-declared .editorconfig policy ("Scope deliberately matches .editorconfig"). Nothing
    declares one for TypeScript, so a gitattributes rule would INVENT a policy and force CRLF
    working trees on CI and every non-Windows contributor -- far larger blast radius than the defect.

    PARTIAL on one criterion: printing the repro command in the JOB OUTPUT needs a
    ci-tier2-advisory.yml edit, which the shadow-window freeze forbids. Command + full root-cause
    explanation documented in docs/procedures/testing-and-code-quality.md instead; the one-line
    workflow change is deferred to after cutover.

    FREEZE-SAFE: no workflow touched.
  </notes>

  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion testable="true">CI and local report the same file count on the same commit</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">The job output names the exact command to reproduce locally</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Remaining unformatted files are either fixed or ignored with a written reason</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>
</task>
